---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Era 1: How It Started (2008–2016)"
  description="The origins of cryptocurrency — from the Cypherpunk movement and pre-Bitcoin experiments through the birth of Bitcoin, early adoption stories, and the altcoin explosion that followed."
>

  <!-- ============================== HERO ============================== -->
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="hero__era-badge">Era 1 of 4</span>
      <h1 id="hero-heading">How It All Started: The Origins of Cryptocurrency&nbsp;(2008–2016)</h1>
      <p class="hero__subtitle">
        Before Bitcoin, there were decades of failed experiments, brilliant cryptographers, and stubborn idealists
        who refused to accept that money had to be controlled by governments. This is the story of how a pseudonymous
        coder finally cracked the puzzle — and why everything that came after was built on the shoulders of those
        who tried first.
      </p>

      <!-- Timeline Bar -->
      <nav class="timeline-bar" aria-label="Key years in Era 1">
        <ol class="timeline-bar__list">
          <li class="timeline-bar__item">
            <a href="#cypherpunks" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">1989–2004</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">Cypherpunks</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="timeline-bar__item timeline-bar__item--highlight">
            <a href="#whitepaper" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">2008</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">Whitepaper</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="timeline-bar__item">
            <a href="#genesis" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">2009</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">Genesis Block</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="timeline-bar__item">
            <a href="#early-adoption" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">2010–2013</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">Early Adoption</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="timeline-bar__item">
            <a href="#altcoins" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">2011–2015</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">Altcoins</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="timeline-bar__item">
            <a href="#dao-hack" class="timeline-bar__link">
              <span class="timeline-bar__year">2016</span>
              <span class="timeline-bar__label">The DAO</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =================== SECTION 1: CYPHERPUNK MOVEMENT =================== -->
  <section class="era-section" id="cypherpunks" aria-labelledby="cypherpunks-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="timeline-section">
        <div class="timeline-track" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="timeline-track__dot"></span>
          <span class="timeline-track__line"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <span class="era-section__date">1989 – 2004</span>
          <h2 id="cypherpunks-heading">The Cypherpunk Movement &amp; Pre-Bitcoin Attempts</h2>
          <p class="era-section__intro">
            Bitcoin didn't appear in a vacuum. For nearly two decades before Satoshi Nakamoto published the
            whitepaper, a loose network of cryptographers, libertarian hackers, and computer scientists had
            been trying to build digital cash. They all wrestled with the same fundamental question: <strong>how
            do you create money that no single entity controls?</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Here's the thing most people get wrong about crypto history — they start with Bitcoin. But if you
            truly want to understand <em>why</em> Bitcoin works, you need to understand why everything before
            it failed. Each failure taught the next builder what to avoid.
          </p>

          <!-- DigiCash -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">1989</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">David Chaum's DigiCash</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              David Chaum, a UC Berkeley cryptographer, launched DigiCash — the first serious attempt at
              creating anonymous digital cash. His system used a cryptographic technique called "blind
              signatures," which let users prove they had money without revealing their identity to the
              bank processing the transaction. Think of it like signing a check inside a sealed carbon-copy
              envelope — the bank stamps it without seeing what's inside.
            </p>
            <p>
              DigiCash was genuinely ahead of its time. Chaum had deals on the table with major banks
              including ING and Deutsche Bank. But he was reportedly difficult to negotiate with, and
              ultimately turned down a deal with Microsoft that could have integrated DigiCash into every
              copy of Windows 95. The company filed for bankruptcy in 1998.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it failed:</strong> Centralized — DigiCash Inc. was the single point of failure. When the
              company went under, the currency died with it. This is the cardinal lesson that echoed through
              every subsequent attempt.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- e-gold -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">1996</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">e-gold</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Douglas Jackson, a Florida oncologist with no formal background in finance, launched e-gold — a
              digital currency backed by real gold stored in vaults in London and Dubai. At its peak in 2006,
              e-gold processed over $2 billion worth of transactions annually and had more than 5 million
              accounts. It was, by any measure, the first successful digital currency.
            </p>
            <p>
              The problem? Because e-gold didn't require identity verification, it became a magnet for money
              launderers, carders (stolen credit card traders), and Ponzi scheme operators. In 2007, the U.S.
              Department of Justice indicted Jackson and his partners for operating an unlicensed money
              transmitting business and facilitating money laundering.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it failed:</strong> Centralized company, centralized gold reserves, and ultimately
              centralized legal liability. The government shut down the servers, and everyone's money became
              inaccessible. If a government can seize your servers and freeze your vaults, you haven't really
              invented independent money.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 Teaching Moment</p>
            <p>
              The e-gold story is one I come back to every year when teaching new students. It perfectly
              illustrates the difference between "digital money" and "decentralized money." E-gold was
              digital — you could send it over the internet. But it was not decentralized — one company
              controlled everything. When regulators came knocking, there was a door to knock on. Bitcoin's
              fundamental design insight was: <strong>eliminate the door entirely.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Hashcash -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">1997</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Adam Back's Hashcash</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Adam Back, a British cryptographer, invented Hashcash as an anti-spam measure. The idea was
              elegant: to send an email, your computer had to solve a small computational puzzle first. This
              "proof of work" took a fraction of a second for a normal user sending a few emails, but made
              mass-spamming prohibitively expensive because a spammer sending millions of emails would need
              massive computing power.
            </p>
            <p>
              Hashcash wasn't a currency, but it introduced the concept that would become the backbone of
              Bitcoin: <strong>proof of work</strong>. The idea that computational effort could serve as a scarce,
              unforgeable resource was revolutionary. Satoshi Nakamoto directly cited Hashcash in the Bitcoin
              whitepaper and adapted its proof-of-work mechanism as the consensus algorithm for mining.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Legacy:</strong> Adam Back went on to co-found Blockstream, one of the most influential
              Bitcoin development companies. He is, to this day, one of the few people some speculate
              could be Satoshi Nakamoto — though Back denies it.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- b-money and Bit Gold -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">1998</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Wei Dai's b-money &amp; Nick Szabo's Bit Gold</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              In 1998, two proposals appeared almost simultaneously that came remarkably close to inventing
              Bitcoin a decade early.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Wei Dai's b-money</strong> proposed a system where participants would maintain their own
              ledgers of account balances and use proof of work to create money. It described both a fully
              decentralized version (where every participant tracks every transaction) and a more practical
              version with designated servers — eerily foreshadowing both Bitcoin's full-node model and
              later light-client approaches.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Nick Szabo's Bit Gold</strong> went even further. Szabo proposed that proof-of-work puzzles
              could be chained together, with each solution timestamped and dependent on the previous one.
              He described a decentralized property registry to track ownership. Sound familiar? It should —
              this is essentially a blockchain, described seven years before the word existed.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why they remained theoretical:</strong> Neither b-money nor Bit Gold solved the "Byzantine
              Generals Problem" — how do you get a distributed network to agree on a single version of truth
              when some participants might be dishonest or offline? Both proposals acknowledged this problem
              but didn't provide a complete, implementable solution. That gap is exactly what Satoshi filled.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--misconception">
            <p class="callout-title">❌ Common Misconception</p>
            <p>
              <strong>"Satoshi Nakamoto invented everything from scratch."</strong> This is flatly wrong. Satoshi
              explicitly cited Hashcash, b-money, and earlier timestamping work in the whitepaper. The genius
              of Bitcoin wasn't inventing new cryptographic primitives — it was combining existing ideas
              (proof of work, hash chains, peer-to-peer networks, public-key cryptography) into a system that
              actually worked. Satoshi was an extraordinary systems integrator, not necessarily a cryptographic
              inventor. The references section of the whitepaper tells you exactly who came before.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Summary box -->
          <div class="summary-box">
            <h3>Why They All Failed — The Common Thread</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>DigiCash:</strong> Single company controlled the system; company goes bankrupt, money disappears</li>
              <li><strong>e-gold:</strong> Centralized reserves could be seized; no KYC attracted criminal use and regulatory action</li>
              <li><strong>Hashcash:</strong> Proof of concept only — proved work could be a resource, but wasn't designed as currency</li>
              <li><strong>b-money &amp; Bit Gold:</strong> Conceptually brilliant but never solved decentralized consensus in practice</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Every pre-Bitcoin project had at least one of these fatal flaws: a central point of control that
              could be shut down, a consensus mechanism that didn't work at scale, or it simply stayed on paper.
              Bitcoin's breakthrough was finding a practical answer to all of these simultaneously.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =================== SECTION 2: BITCOIN WHITEPAPER =================== -->
  <section class="era-section era-section--alt" id="whitepaper" aria-labelledby="whitepaper-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="timeline-section">
        <div class="timeline-track" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="timeline-track__dot timeline-track__dot--highlight"></span>
          <span class="timeline-track__line"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <span class="era-section__date">October 31, 2008</span>
          <h2 id="whitepaper-heading">The Bitcoin Whitepaper</h2>
          <p class="era-section__intro">
            On Halloween 2008 — while the world was reeling from the collapse of Lehman Brothers and the
            worst financial crisis since the Great Depression — a pseudonymous figure calling themselves
            <strong>Satoshi Nakamoto</strong> posted a nine-page document to the Cryptography Mailing List.
            The subject line read: <em>"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System."</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            That nine-page paper is, in my opinion, one of the most consequential documents of the 21st
            century. Not because of its length or complexity — it's actually remarkably readable — but
            because it solved a problem that computer scientists had been stuck on for decades: how to
            create digital scarcity without a central authority.
          </p>

          <div class="whitepaper-breakdown">
            <h3>The Key Innovations, Explained Simply</h3>

            <div class="innovation-card">
              <div class="innovation-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">📒</div>
              <div class="innovation-card__body">
                <h4>The Decentralized Ledger (Blockchain)</h4>
                <p>
                  <strong>The analogy:</strong> Imagine a town where there's no bank, but every resident
                  keeps an identical copy of a giant notebook that records every transaction ever made.
                  When Alice pays Bob, she announces it to the whole town, and everyone writes it down.
                  If someone tries to cheat by altering their copy, it won't match everyone else's, and
                  the town ignores the forged version. That's a blockchain — a shared, append-only record
                  that no single person controls.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="innovation-card">
              <div class="innovation-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">⛏️</div>
              <div class="innovation-card__body">
                <h4>Proof of Work (Mining)</h4>
                <p>
                  <strong>The analogy:</strong> Someone has to decide which transactions get written into
                  the next page of that notebook, and when. Satoshi's answer: make them compete. Imagine
                  the townspeople racing to solve a jigsaw puzzle. The first person to finish gets to write
                  the next page and earns a reward (new coins). The puzzle is hard to solve but easy for
                  everyone else to verify — you can instantly see a finished jigsaw is correct. This is
                  mining, and it's how Bitcoin decides who adds the next block without needing a central
                  authority to choose.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="innovation-card">
              <div class="innovation-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">🔄</div>
              <div class="innovation-card__body">
                <h4>The Double-Spend Solution</h4>
                <p>
                  <strong>The problem:</strong> Digital files can be copied. If I email you a photo, I still
                  have the photo. So how do you prevent someone from spending the same digital coin twice?
                  Before Bitcoin, the only answer was to have a central server track balances (like a bank).
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Satoshi's solution:</strong> By combining the blockchain (everyone sees every
                  transaction) with proof of work (transactions are locked in by computational effort),
                  it becomes practically impossible to spend the same coin twice. You'd have to redo all
                  the computational work faster than the entire rest of the network — which, as the
                  network grows, becomes astronomically unlikely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="innovation-card">
              <div class="innovation-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">🔢</div>
              <div class="innovation-card__body">
                <h4>The 21 Million Cap</h4>
                <p>
                  Satoshi hard-coded a maximum supply of 21 million bitcoins into the protocol. New coins
                  are created as mining rewards, but that reward halves approximately every four years
                  (every 210,000 blocks). It started at 50 BTC per block in 2009, dropped to 25 in 2012,
                  12.5 in 2016, 6.25 in 2020, and 3.125 in 2024. The last bitcoin will be mined around
                  the year 2140.
                </p>
                <p>
                  This fixed supply is what gives Bitcoin its "digital gold" narrative. Unlike dollars or
                  euros, no government or central bank can decide to print more. The monetary policy is
                  set in code, transparent, and unchangeable.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 Teaching Moment</p>
            <p>
              I always tell students: the Bitcoin whitepaper is only nine pages. I've graded undergraduate
              papers longer than that. If you're serious about understanding crypto, read it. You don't
              need a PhD — Satoshi wrote it to be understood. The hardest concept (the proof-of-work
              consensus mechanism) is explained in about two paragraphs. You can find it at
              <strong>bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf</strong>. Reading the original source will teach you more than
              a hundred Medium articles.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Genesis Block -->
          <article class="milestone-card milestone-card--featured" id="genesis">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Jan 3, 2009</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">The Genesis Block</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              At 18:15:05 UTC on January 3, 2009, Satoshi Nakamoto mined the first-ever Bitcoin block —
              Block 0, known as the Genesis Block. Embedded in its coinbase transaction was a message that
              has become one of the most iconic texts in cryptocurrency history:
            </p>
            <blockquote class="genesis-quote">
              "The Times 03/Jan/2009 Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks"
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              This was the headline from the front page of <em>The Times</em> of London that day. Whether
              Satoshi included it as a timestamp proof (to show the block wasn't pre-mined before that date)
              or as a political statement about why Bitcoin was needed — or both — has been debated endlessly.
              I think it was clearly both. It simultaneously served a technical verification purpose and
              made a pointed commentary on the failures of the existing financial system.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Genesis Block had a quirk: its 50 BTC reward is unspendable due to a peculiarity in
              the code (the transaction isn't in the spendable transaction database). Whether this was
              intentional or a minor oversight by Satoshi has never been confirmed. Those 50 bitcoins
              sit in their address to this day, untouched and untouchable.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--misconception">
            <p class="callout-title">❌ Common Misconception</p>
            <p>
              <strong>"We know nothing about Satoshi Nakamoto."</strong> We actually know quite a lot from
              the forensic trail. Satoshi posted on forums (bitcointalk.org), exchanged private emails with
              developers like Hal Finney and Gavin Andresen, and wrote code in a specific style. From
              timestamp analysis, Satoshi likely lived in a GMT-timezone and slept roughly British hours.
              The writing style is fluent English with occasional British spellings ("colour," "favour").
              What we don't know is their legal identity — and after over 15 years, it seems Satoshi wants
              it that way.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =================== SECTION 3: EARLY ADOPTION =================== -->
  <section class="era-section" id="early-adoption" aria-labelledby="adoption-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="timeline-section">
        <div class="timeline-track" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="timeline-track__dot"></span>
          <span class="timeline-track__line"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <span class="era-section__date">2009 – 2014</span>
          <h2 id="adoption-heading">Early Adoption Stories</h2>
          <p class="era-section__intro">
            A protocol is just theory until people actually use it. The early years of Bitcoin were defined
            by a handful of true believers, one legendary pizza order, a dark marketplace that proved
            digital cash actually worked, and a catastrophic exchange hack that taught the entire ecosystem
            an expensive lesson about trust.
          </p>

          <!-- Hal Finney -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Jan 12, 2009</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Hal Finney — The First Bitcoin Transaction</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Nine days after the Genesis Block, cryptographer <strong>Hal Finney</strong> received 10 BTC from Satoshi
              Nakamoto in what is considered the first-ever person-to-person Bitcoin transaction (Block 170).
              Finney was a legendary figure in the cypherpunk community — he had worked at PGP Corporation,
              created the first reusable proof-of-work system (RPOW) in 2004, and was one of the first people
              to engage with Satoshi's proposal on the Cryptography Mailing List.
            </p>
            <p>
              On January 11, 2009 — the day before his historic transaction — Finney posted on Twitter:
              <em>"Running bitcoin."</em> Those two words are now considered one of the most historically
              significant tweets in technology history.
            </p>
            <p>
              Tragically, Finney was diagnosed with ALS (Lou Gehrig's disease) in 2009 and passed away on
              August 28, 2014. He was cryopreserved by the Alcor Life Extension Foundation. His bitcoins
              remain in cold storage. Finney is another individual who has been suggested as a possible
              Satoshi (he lived just blocks from a man named Dorian Satoshi Nakamoto in Temple City,
              California), but the evidence is circumstantial at best.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- Pizza Day -->
          <article class="milestone-card milestone-card--featured">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">May 22, 2010</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">The Bitcoin Pizza — 10,000 BTC for Two Pies</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              On May 22, 2010, a programmer named <strong>Laszlo Hanyecz</strong> posted on the BitcoinTalk
              forum offering 10,000 BTC to anyone who would order him two large pizzas. At the time, Bitcoin
              had no established exchange rate, and those 10,000 BTC were worth roughly $41. A user named
              "jercos" (Jeremy Sturdivant) took him up on the offer and ordered two Papa John's pizzas
              delivered to Hanyecz's home in Jacksonville, Florida.
            </p>
            <p>
              This event is now celebrated annually as <strong>"Bitcoin Pizza Day"</strong> on May 22nd. At
              Bitcoin's all-time high near $69,000 in November 2021, those 10,000 BTC would have been worth
              approximately <strong>$690 million</strong>. It's the most expensive pizza purchase in history
              by an almost absurd margin.
            </p>
            <p>
              But here's the nuance that the memes miss: Hanyecz doesn't regret it. As he later explained,
              someone needed to establish that Bitcoin could be exchanged for real goods. Without people like
              Laszlo willing to actually <em>spend</em> Bitcoin, it would have remained a curiosity on a
              mailing list. This transaction proved that BTC had real economic utility. Someone had to go first.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 Teaching Moment</p>
            <p>
              Students always laugh at Laszlo, but I ask them this: if nobody ever spends a currency, is it
              actually a currency? A "money" that everyone hoards and nobody uses is just a collectible.
              The pizza transaction was economically rational at the time — 10,000 tokens with no established
              value for two real pizzas was arguably a great deal for Laszlo. History is always 20/20, and the
              fact that Bitcoin eventually became worth thousands per coin doesn't make his decision wrong in
              context. <strong>Every new technology needs early adopters willing to use it before it's cool.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Silk Road -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">2011 – 2013</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Silk Road — The Dark Market That Proved Bitcoin Worked</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              In February 2011, <strong>Ross Ulbricht</strong>, operating under the pseudonym "Dread Pirate
              Roberts," launched the Silk Road — an anonymous online marketplace accessible only through the
              Tor network. It used Bitcoin as its exclusive currency. While the Silk Road sold everything from
              books to art, it was overwhelmingly known for facilitating the sale of illegal drugs.
            </p>
            <p>
              Here's the uncomfortable truth that the crypto industry doesn't always like to acknowledge: Silk
              Road was, for a period, the single most important driver of Bitcoin demand. It provided Bitcoin
              with its first real-world use case where BTC wasn't just a novelty — it was <em>necessary</em>.
              You couldn't buy products on Silk Road with credit cards or PayPal. Bitcoin was the only option,
              and that exclusivity drove adoption among a niche but economically active user base.
            </p>
            <p>
              The FBI shut down Silk Road in October 2013 and arrested Ulbricht, who was later convicted on
              charges including conspiracy to traffic narcotics, money laundering, and running a continuing
              criminal enterprise. He was sentenced to life in prison without parole — a sentence many in the
              crypto community have argued was disproportionately severe.
            </p>
            <p>
              At the time of the seizure, the FBI confiscated approximately 144,000 BTC from Silk Road wallets.
              The U.S. government later auctioned off these bitcoins in batches — venture capitalist Tim Draper
              famously purchased nearly 30,000 BTC in one such auction in 2014.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 Teaching Moment</p>
            <p>
              The Silk Road story teaches a crucial lesson about technology neutrality. The internet itself was
              once condemned because it enabled access to illegal content. Email was criticized because it
              enabled fraud. Bitcoin is a tool — and like all tools, it can be used for both legitimate and
              illegitimate purposes. Today, blockchain analytics firms like Chainalysis can actually trace Bitcoin
              transactions far more effectively than cash, which is why Bitcoin has largely fallen out of favor
              with sophisticated criminals who now prefer privacy coins or simply old-fashioned $100 bills.
              <strong>Bitcoin's blockchain is a permanent, public record — it's actually terrible for crime.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Mt. Gox -->
          <article class="milestone-card milestone-card--danger">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Feb 2014</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Mt. Gox — The Catastrophic Exchange Collapse</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Mt. Gox (short for "Magic: The Gathering Online eXchange" — yes, really — it started as a
              trading card exchange) was the world's largest Bitcoin exchange from 2011 to 2014, handling
              over 70% of all Bitcoin transactions at its peak.
            </p>
            <p>
              In February 2014, Mt. Gox suddenly halted all withdrawals, went offline, and filed for bankruptcy.
              CEO Mark Karpelès revealed that approximately <strong>850,000 BTC</strong> — worth around
              $450 million at the time — had been stolen through a hack that had apparently been draining the
              exchange for years without detection. About 200,000 BTC were later found in old wallets, reducing
              the loss to approximately 650,000 BTC, but the damage was devastating.
            </p>
            <p>
              Hundreds of thousands of users lost their funds. The Bitcoin price, which had risen from around $13
              to over $1,100 in 2013, crashed below $400. Many mainstream commentators wrote Bitcoin's obituary.
              The Mt. Gox bankruptcy proceedings dragged on for over a decade, with creditors finally beginning
              to receive partial repayments in 2024.
            </p>
            <p>
              The hack exploited a vulnerability related to "transaction malleability" — a subtle flaw in how
              Bitcoin transaction IDs were calculated that allowed attackers to alter a transaction's ID after
              it was broadcast but before it was confirmed. This made it possible to trick the exchange into
              thinking withdrawals had failed and resending them. The fix for transaction malleability was
              eventually incorporated into Bitcoin through the Segregated Witness (SegWit) upgrade in 2017.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 Teaching Moment</p>
            <p>
              Mt. Gox gave us the mantra that every Bitcoin veteran knows by heart: <strong>"Not your keys,
              not your coins."</strong> When you leave cryptocurrency on an exchange, you don't actually hold
              it — the exchange does. You have an IOU. If the exchange is hacked, goes bankrupt, or simply
              decides to freeze your account, you have no recourse. This lesson was reinforced in 2022 when
              FTX collapsed. Self-custody (holding your own private keys in a hardware wallet) is the only way
              to truly own your cryptocurrency. It's less convenient, but Mt. Gox showed that convenience
              comes with existential risk.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =================== SECTION 4: ALTCOIN EXPLOSION =================== -->
  <section class="era-section era-section--alt" id="altcoins" aria-labelledby="altcoins-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="timeline-section">
        <div class="timeline-track" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="timeline-track__dot"></span>
          <span class="timeline-track__line"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-content">
          <span class="era-section__date">2011 – 2016</span>
          <h2 id="altcoins-heading">The Altcoin Explosion</h2>
          <p class="era-section__intro">
            Once Satoshi proved that decentralized digital money was possible, the obvious next question was:
            can we build something better? Or at least <em>different?</em> Between 2011 and 2016, hundreds
            of alternative cryptocurrencies (altcoins) launched, each claiming to improve on Bitcoin in some
            way. Most were worthless clones. A few genuinely changed the industry.
          </p>

          <!-- Namecoin -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Apr 2011</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Namecoin — The First Altcoin</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Namecoin holds the distinction of being the first-ever alternative cryptocurrency, launched in
              April 2011 as a fork of Bitcoin's code. But it wasn't just a copy — Namecoin had a genuinely
              novel purpose. It aimed to create a decentralized domain name system (DNS) that couldn't be
              censored by governments or ICANN. Domains ending in <code>.bit</code> were registered directly
              on the Namecoin blockchain.
            </p>
            <p>
              The project demonstrated that blockchain technology could be used for more than just money —
              it could store and transfer arbitrary data, like domain name registrations. While Namecoin
              itself never achieved widespread adoption (the <code>.bit</code> domains aren't recognized
              by standard browsers), its concept influenced everything from Ethereum's naming service (ENS)
              to Handshake and Unstoppable Domains.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- Litecoin -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Oct 2011</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Litecoin — "Silver to Bitcoin's Gold"</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Charlie Lee, a former Google engineer, launched Litecoin in October 2011 with a clear pitch:
              it was Bitcoin, but faster and lighter. Litecoin used a different mining algorithm (Scrypt
              instead of SHA-256), produced blocks every 2.5 minutes instead of Bitcoin's 10, and had a
              maximum supply of 84 million coins (4x Bitcoin's cap).
            </p>
            <p>
              Lee famously positioned Litecoin as the "silver to Bitcoin's gold" — a complement, not a
              competitor. It was meant for smaller, everyday transactions where Bitcoin's slower confirmation
              times and higher fees were impractical. Litecoin also served as a testing ground for Bitcoin
              improvements — it adopted SegWit before Bitcoin did, demonstrating the upgrade's viability
              at scale.
            </p>
            <p>
              Litecoin remains one of the longest-surviving altcoins, which in cryptocurrency is itself an
              achievement. The vast majority of altcoins launched in this era are dead.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- Ripple/XRP -->
          <article class="milestone-card">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">2012</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Ripple / XRP — The Bank-Friendly Heresy</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              If Bitcoin was born from cypherpunk ideology — distrust of banks, rejection of central control —
              then Ripple was its philosophical opposite. Founded by Chris Larsen and Jed McCaleb, Ripple
              Labs created XRP and the RippleNet protocol specifically to serve banks and financial
              institutions.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rather than replacing the banking system, Ripple aimed to make cross-border bank transfers
              faster and cheaper. Traditional international wire transfers via SWIFT can take 3–5 business
              days and cost $25–50. Ripple's protocol promised settlement in 3–5 <em>seconds</em> at a
              fraction of a penny.
            </p>
            <p>
              The crypto purist community never fully accepted XRP. It was pre-mined (all 100 billion XRP
              were created at launch, with Ripple Labs retaining roughly 60% in escrow), it didn't use
              proof of work, and it was controlled by a corporation. In December 2020, the SEC sued Ripple
              Labs alleging that XRP was an unregistered security — a case that became a landmark battle
              in crypto regulation and was substantially resolved in Ripple's favor regarding secondary
              market sales in 2023.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--misconception">
            <p class="callout-title">❌ Common Misconception</p>
            <p>
              <strong>"All cryptocurrencies are trying to replace government money."</strong> Not even close.
              Ripple/XRP was designed to help banks move money faster. Namecoin was about domain names.
              Ethereum (coming next) was about programmable contracts. The cryptocurrency ecosystem is far
              more diverse than "internet money." Different projects have wildly different goals, designs,
              and philosophies. Evaluating every crypto project by whether it's "better money than Bitcoin"
              misses the point for the vast majority of them.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Ethereum -->
          <article class="milestone-card milestone-card--featured">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Jul 30, 2015</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">Ethereum — The Smart Contracts Revolution</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              In late 2013, a 19-year-old Russian-Canadian programmer named <strong>Vitalik Buterin</strong>
              published a whitepaper proposing a blockchain that could do more than just track money. Bitcoin's
              scripting language was deliberately limited — Satoshi designed it that way for security. Buterin
              argued that a blockchain with a Turing-complete programming language could serve as a "world
              computer" — a decentralized platform where anyone could deploy applications that run exactly as
              programmed, without any possibility of censorship, downtime, or third-party interference.
            </p>
            <p>
              After a wildly successful crowdsale in mid-2014 that raised approximately 31,500 BTC (around
              $18 million at the time), Ethereum launched its mainnet on July 30, 2015. The name "Ethereum"
              was inspired by the concept of the "luminiferous ether" — a hypothetical medium that
              19th-century physicists believed permeated all of space.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The key innovation was the smart contract.</strong> A smart contract is a program that
              lives on the blockchain and automatically executes when predefined conditions are met. Think of
              it as a vending machine made of code: you insert the right input (coins + selection), and the
              machine <em>must</em> give you the output (your snack). No human is needed to "approve" the
              transaction. No one can override it. The code is the final authority.
            </p>
            <p>
              This unlocked entirely new categories of application: decentralized exchanges, lending platforms,
              NFTs, DAOs (decentralized autonomous organizations), and eventually the entire DeFi (decentralized
              finance) ecosystem. Ethereum didn't just add a feature to cryptocurrency — it expanded what
              blockchain technology could fundamentally be used for.
            </p>
          </article>

          <!-- The DAO -->
          <article class="milestone-card milestone-card--danger" id="dao-hack">
            <div class="milestone-card__header">
              <span class="milestone-card__year">Jun 2016</span>
              <h3 class="milestone-card__title">The DAO Hack — $60 Million and a Fork in the Road</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Less than a year after Ethereum's launch came its first existential crisis. In April 2016, a
              project called "The DAO" (Decentralized Autonomous Organization) launched on Ethereum — essentially
              a crowdfunded venture capital fund controlled entirely by smart contract code. Token holders could
              vote on which projects to fund. It raised a staggering 12.7 million ETH (approximately $150 million
              at the time), making it the largest crowdfund in history at that point.
            </p>
            <p>
              On June 17, 2016, an attacker exploited a vulnerability in The DAO's smart contract code — a
              "reentrancy bug" that allowed them to repeatedly withdraw funds before the contract updated its
              balance. Over several hours, the attacker drained approximately 3.6 million ETH (~$60 million)
              into a child contract they controlled.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Ethereum community faced an agonizing choice: do nothing and let the attacker keep the money
              (preserving the principle that "code is law" and the blockchain is immutable), or roll back the
              transaction by forking the blockchain (saving the money but setting a precedent that the community
              could override smart contract outcomes when they didn't like the result).
            </p>
            <p>
              After heated debate, the community voted to hard fork. On July 20, 2016, Ethereum split into two
              chains: <strong>Ethereum (ETH)</strong>, the forked version where The DAO hack was reversed, and
              <strong>Ethereum Classic (ETC)</strong>, the original chain where the hack stood. Most of the
              community, developers, and the Ethereum Foundation followed the forked chain (ETH), which is what
              people today simply call "Ethereum."
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="callout callout--teaching">
            <p class="callout-title">🎓 What I Wish I Knew Then</p>
            <p>
              The DAO hack is the case study I teach every single year because it raises questions that still
              have no clean answers. If "code is law" on a blockchain, does that mean an exploit is just
              "using the code as written" rather than theft? If the community can vote to reverse transactions,
              how is that different from a bank reversing a fraudulent charge? And if Ethereum can fork once
              to reverse a hack, why can't it fork again for any reason?
            </p>
            <p>
              In practice, Ethereum has never done another rollback fork. The DAO was a unique situation — the
              funds were locked in a time-delay contract, the amount was existentially large (nearly 15% of all
              ETH at the time), and the ecosystem was young enough that near-unanimous agreement was possible.
              <strong>But the precedent exists, and that's philosophically uncomfortable.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =================== ERA SUMMARY =================== -->
  <section class="era-section" id="era-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="era-summary-box">
        <h2 id="summary-heading">Era 1 in Perspective</h2>
        <p>
          From 2008 to 2016, cryptocurrency went from a nine-page whitepaper posted to a mailing list
          to a multi-billion-dollar ecosystem with hundreds of projects, thousands of developers, and
          millions of users. Bitcoin proved that decentralized money was possible. Ethereum proved that
          decentralized <em>computing</em> was possible. And a series of spectacular failures — Mt. Gox,
          Silk Road, The DAO — taught the ecosystem painful but essential lessons about security, custody,
          and the tension between idealism and pragmatism.
        </p>
        <p>
          But by the end of 2016, crypto was still largely a niche pursuit. Most people had heard of Bitcoin
          but dismissed it as fake internet money or a tool for criminals. The real mainstream moment — the
          2017 ICO mania, the institutional adoption, the regulatory battles — was still ahead.
        </p>
        <div class="era-summary-stats">
          <div class="era-stat">
            <span class="era-stat__value">$0 → $16B</span>
            <span class="era-stat__label">Total crypto market cap growth (2009–2016)</span>
